speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I should like to make a few comments on the supplementary estimates introduced today by the minister and which have no bearing on any votes regarding grants to gold mines.
Mr. Speaker, if I may, I should like to say a few words about the situation of the mining towns in northwesternn Quebec, in particular Val-D'Or, Malartic, Cadillac, Chapais, Rouyn-Noranda and Chibougamau. These seven municipalities exist today mainly because of mining operations, which provide employment to 2,000 workers. They were established around the 30's. Millions of dollars were spent to provide the citizens with adequate public services and to make of these communities well-organized municipalities.
If the Minister of Finance (Mr. Benson) has not yet visited Abitibi, I am pleased to invite him to do so and I hope that he will come during the 70's. Several ministers of this government have already paid a short visit to Abitibi. They were surprised to find that our municipalities were modern and equipped with the same facilities as large urban centres in Canada.
Mr. Speaker, lately I received seven resolutions from city councils and school boards of my area asking the government to continue

March 24, 1970
Supplementary Estimates the grants to gold mines after the 70's and even asking for an increase. Several letters were sent for that purpose to the Minister of Industry, Trade and Commerce (Mr. Pepin) and to the Minister of Manpower and Immigration (Mr. MacEachen).
Mr. Speaker, here is one of the resolutions which was passed by these seven municipalities.
Whereas gold has for thousands of years been recognized as a precious metal and been sought after by all the peoples of the world;
Whereas Canada, as many other countries, has its gold-mining industry;
Whereas considerable capital has been invested to build up that important industry in our country;
Whereas, in 1939 there were 232 gold mines in Canada;
Whereas, 30 years later, namely in 1969, only some thirty of them were still operating;
Whereas that industry is threatened with extinction in the near future;
Whereas well-organized cities were built near those mines, for the miners and their families;
Whereas those cities were built in a large measure with funds provided by small investors;
Whereas the government of Canada recognized, in 1947, that the closing down of mines as a result of higher operation costs penalized and even ruined the defenseless small investor;
Whereas the government of Canada started, in 1947, to pay subsidies to the gold mines threatened with having to close down;
Whereas almost all Canadian gold mines operating today would have closed down had it not been for federal grants;
Whereas operating costs are still going up and subsidies have not been increased since 1958;
Whereas the Gold Mining Assistance Act will remain in force only to the end of 1970;
Be it resolved as moved by alderman Adrien Paquin, seconded by alderman Wilfrid Asselin, that the municipal council, in the face of so serious a situation, ask the federal government not only that assistance to the gold mines be maintained after 1970 but that it be increased to enable those mines to continue operating and to save from bankruptcy those municipalities which depend on them.
Mr. Speaker, I have just read the resolution approved by seven municipalities in my riding of Villeneuve. Everyone knows that the Emergency Gold Mining Assistanct Act expires at the end of December 1970. I beg the Minister and the government to study thoroughly all the elements related to that industry.
In January 1934, the United States increased the price of gold. It was at that time $20.67 per ounce and it went up to $35 per ounce. That rise led to an increase in prospection and prospectors thus discovered mines of all kinds. A rise in grants after 1970

would be beneficial, I think, to the government and the Canadian people. No doubt such a decision would be the beginning of a new development in prospection throughout Canada.
The word "emergency" was well chosen when the government passed that measure and it is still appropriate today. Without that measure, all gold mines could not survive, which would be unfortunate in a just society, as the Prime Minister (Mr. Trudeau) so justly said during the 1968 election campaign.
In my area, 2,000 miners earn their living that way. They pay every year to the government large amounts of money in the form of taxes. If the government compared the income of employees and employers who must rely on gold mines to live, to the grants that amount to about $15 million, they would find out that those grants are largely made up through taxes paid by the workers.
The implementation of the Emergency Gold Mining Assistance Act has enabled this country to overcome the economic difficulties created by the rise in operation costs and the stability in the price of gold.
Mining is one of the oldest industries in Canada. It goes back to the first French explorers and settlers, some 350 years ago. The first ore discovery was probably made by a mining engineer accompanying Samuel de Champlain during one of his exploration journeys in the New World, at the beginning of the 17th century. Champlain himself mentioned the existence of silver in Nova Scotia and of copper in the Gaspe peninsula.
It is, however, mainly in the course of the last century that the most interesting discoveries were made. Three important events were the highlights of this time: the building of the railways, the Klondike gold rush and the disovery of a silver field at Cobalt in Ontario.
In 1970, the Canadian government will pay many millions of dollars in subsidies to the western farmers, in order to prevent them from producing wheat. I want to be quite clear about that: I do not mind whether the government pays subsidies to the farmers for Canadians certainly need farmers to grow wheat.
The same could apply to unsold milk. But we could certainly sell all the gold we produce to the United States. Let me quote here from a newspaper article written by Mr. Amedee Gaudreault, which outlines very
March 24, 1970	COMMONS
well, in my view, the situation now prevailing in the gold mines and I quote:
Some experts have told me that gold mining operations have not changed very much over the last ten years and that no major improvements are foreseen in the near future. From 1950 to 1960 the important progress that was achieved then made it possible to increase the production of a miner by about 50 per cent, but that was all.
Besides, the industry never stopped declining: production amounted to 4,541,962 ounces in 1955, to 3,606,031 ounces in 1965 and to about 2,500,000 ounces last year. As for the miners, they numbered 22,491 in 1950, 18,032 in 1955, 12,520 in 1965 and 7,800 in 1969; nearly 75 per cent of them are employed in northeastern Ontario and northwestern Quebec, where is located the famous "belt", comprising Timmins, Porcupine, Kirkland Lake, No-randa and Val d'Or.
[DOT] (5:00 p.m.)

A committee of six municipal members was formed to study the possibility of presenting a brief to the Minister of Energy, Mines and Resources (Mr. Greene). Preparing a brief every three years is certainly an expensive proposition for the municipalities concerned because the brief alone would cost approximately $15,000 and there would be the added expense of the delegates. In closing, may I point out to the minister that we are trying to help the government find a way to solve the problem of unemployment in Canada. Let us not add to the half million already waiting for work who are on the rolls of the unemployed. It would be helpful if the miners in my district could find employment. I, therefore, hope the government will support our proposition to continue the subsidies for 1970 and after. The government should make its decision as soon as possible because the municipalities in my district are very nervous and do not know whether the government will pay the subsidy on gold mining. I hope the government will decide this matter very shortly, because these municipalities could receive no greater gift than the knowledge the government is deciding the question of these subsidies in the very near future.
